<TopicPage slug="subjects/sports-science/intermediate/muscles-and-energy-systems">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The energy systems are <strong>three engines in one chassis</strong> — instantaneous,
    short-burst and long-haul — with the intensity dial setting the mix. Training is engine
    selection: you get better at exactly what you practise, in the currency each system spends.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three engines, the overlap, training targets, fibre types" widget="equationStep" config={energyCfg} />

  <h2>Details that matter to practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lactate rehabilitated:</strong> the old "lactic acid causes fatigue" story is wrong — lactate is a fuel shuttled to the heart and brain (Brooks\'s work); the burn is acidosis from ATP hydrolysis, and lactate threshold is a TRAINABLE performance marker (the testing lesson measures it in mmol/L).</li>
    <li><strong>VO₂max is the ceiling, economy is the size of the room:</strong> elite distance runners differ more in running economy (oxygen used per km) than in VO₂max — trainable technique (the biomechanics lesson) buys what genetics won\'t.</li>
    <li><strong>EPO and altitude are the same mechanism:</strong> less oxygen → more red blood cells (legal at altitude, illegal as a drug — the anti-doping line is a rule, not a chemistry).</li>
    <li><strong>The creatine evidence:</strong> the most-studied supplement in sports science — small but real gains in repeated high-intensity work, no credible harms at standard doses (the nutrition lesson\'s evidence bar, applied).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Brooks on lactate; McArdle, Katch & Katch, Exercise Physiology; the biology lesson\'s respiration chapter.</p>
</TopicPage>
